const intro={"n":12,"title":"Fierro's Son Describes Orphanhood and Solitary Imprisonment","nums":[660,678,708],"summary":"One of Fierro's sons now takes up the story. He remembers growing up without family protection, working as a hired hand and being accused in a killing that was never clearly resolved. He and two others are sent to the penitentiary. His long account focuses on profound isolation, silence, the loss of conversation and the psychological suffering of confinement. He laments not having learned to read and argues that speech and friendship are among humanity's greatest gifts. His narrative does not establish that he committed the crime.","people":"One of Martín Fierro's sons as the new first-person narrator; a judge; two other accused men; warders; the speaker's absent relatives.","themes":"Orphanhood and abandonment; accusations and criminal justice; solitary confinement; the right to speak; literacy and dignity; testimony from the next generation.","words":"güerfano (vernacular form of huérfano, orphan); pión (peón, hired worker); sumario (preliminary legal proceedings); penitenciaria (penitentiary); grillo (shackle); presidario (prisoner).","questions":["Who is telling the story, and how is his voice different from his father's?","What is known and unknown about the killing that led to the arrest?","Why does the speaker regard isolation as a worse punishment than physical shackles?","What does he say about the importance of speech and friendship?","What educational disadvantage does he regret while imprisoned?"],"analysis":"The son's description of legal proceedings does not authorize a verdict of guilt: the canto explicitly leaves the killing unclear. His powerful account centers on deprivation of human contact. His claim that some prison authorities behaved decently also keeps the narrative from being a simple portrayal of every officer as cruel. Study the difference between the narrator's direct experience and his broader claims about justice.","related":"/en/library/themes-and-analysis/","relatedTitle":"Loss, justice and the voices of the poem"};
